><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251112100553+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="019b8e30-a02b-4f90-a14a-4402f5de002e" date="D:20251112100557+10'00'" name="207b934a-a8ed-4d90-a4d4-49963d5869d6" icon="Comment" page="7" rect="310.357422,77.410538,334.357422,101.410538" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Drop landings and hop jumps do not replicate or provide&#xD;realistic measures of patellar tendon loading when compared&#xD;to that of spike jumps. From this study it is clear that assessment&#xD;tasks such as those used for RTP need to incorporate </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>more representative movements (landing mechanics), to generate&#xD;greater accuracy of assessment measures. AR opens&#xD;a world of possibilities for future assessment applications&#xD;because information can be manipulated in ways that are not&#xD;possible for an isolated real-world spike jump was performed.&#xD;As this is the first research of this nature, it is imperative that&#xD;future research further investigates the ability of AR in enhancing&#xD;skill transferral and RLD across environments, and the&#xD;benefit in doing so.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="7" rect="609.448975,9.410538,793.448975,101.410538"
/></text
